# Destructive device-SD operations require permission first

The Teensy SD card holds state that is **not** reconstructible by the agent: the
current workspace, recovery checkpoints, and saved loop slots. Ask the user for
explicit permission **in the same session, naming the exact operation** before
running any of these.

## Never do this unprompted

- `!QUARANTINE_WORKSPACE` (or `-D BOOT_QUARANTINE_WORKSPACE=1`) — moves
  `/MidiLooper/current` to `/MidiLooper/current.bad.<millis>` and
  `/MidiLooper/recovery/checkpoints` to `checkpoints.bad.<millis>`.
- `!REV_NUKE_SETS`, `!REV_CLEANUP`, or any command that deletes sets/slots.
- Any `SD.remove` / `SD.rename` / `rm` / `mv` reaching the card through a host
  mount, a helper script, or new firmware.

**Quarantine is reversible, but only through the stamp.** `!RESTORE_WORKSPACE`
inverts it: `!RESTORE_WORKSPACE list` prints every quarantined tree without
renaming anything, `!RESTORE_WORKSPACE <stamp>` restores that one, and a bare
`!RESTORE_WORKSPACE` restores the newest. It renames only — the live tree is
parked as `.bad.<now>`, never deleted. **Always run `list` first.** The stamp is
device `millis()` at rename time, so it cannot be derived from the host, and the
card accumulates trees across boots (observed: 20 `current` / 21 `checkpoints`
trees after routine use of the quarantine script). Never assume the newest tree
is the one you just created.

## How to ask

State the command, every path that will move, and what the user loses:

> The Clear gate is stuck on `Persistence drain stuck after 256 iterations`.
> I want to run `!QUARANTINE_WORKSPACE`, which renames
> `/MidiLooper/current` → `/MidiLooper/current.bad.<stamp>` and
> `/MidiLooper/recovery/checkpoints` → `checkpoints.bad.<stamp>`. Loop slots
> under `/MidiLooper/sets/` are untouched. The firmware cannot rename them back;
> restoring means mounting the card and renaming by hand. OK?

Then wait for a yes. A stuck HITL precondition, a failed capture, or a fault is
**not** permission.

## When it is allowed

Only after the user agrees, and say in the same turn exactly what moved so it can
be restored. Record the resulting `.bad.<stamp>` paths in the session journal.

Prefer a non-mutating route first: reboot, re-upload, an already-empty scratch
slot, or a host-side fix. Quarantine is a last resort.
